------
Read files - https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/read_files

----
Parquet: It’s a columnar storage format that is self-describing. That means the schema (column names, data types, etc.) is embedded inside the file itself.
When you query a Parquet file, the engine (Spark, Hive, DuckDB, etc.) can automatically read the schema without you having to manually specify it.
This is one of the big advantages of Parquet — it’s efficient and schema-aware.

CSV: A CSV file is just plain text with rows and columns separated by delimiters (commas, tabs, etc.). 
It doesn’t carry any metadata about column types. 
So when you load a CSV into a database or query engine, you usually need to define the schema explicitly (e.g., which columns are strings, integers, dates). Otherwise, the system will treat everything as text,
which can cause issues with performance and correctness.


------

SELECT *
FROM parquet.`/path/to/file.parquet`
Spark SQL lets you query Parquet files directly using the file path.



-----


SELECT *
FROM read_files(
       DA.paths_working_dir || '/xml_demo_files/example_1_data.xml',
       format => "xml",
       rowTag => 'book',
       schema => '''
            _id INT, 
            author STRING, 
            title STRING
          ''',
       rescueddatacolumn => '_rescued_data'
     );

The function read_files is part of Databricks’ Auto Loader / file ingestion APIs, which internally leverage Spark’s DataFrame reader capabilities.

-- Drop the table if it exists for demonstration purposes
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS books_bronze_xml;


-- Create the Delta table
CREATE TABLE books_bronze_xml 
AS
SELECT
  _id AS book_id,
  * EXCEPT (_id),
  current_timestamp AS ingestion_timestamp,
  _metadata.file_name AS source_file
FROM read_files(
       DA.paths_working_dir || '/xml_demo_files/example_1_data.xml',
       format => "xml",
       rowTag => 'book',
       schema => '''
            _id INT, 
            author STRING, 
            title STRING
          ''',
       rescuedDataColumn => '_rescued_data'
     );

---

-- View the datatypes of the columns 
DESCRIBE books_bronze_xml

---
DESCRIBE VOLUME dbacademy_ecommerce.v01.raw;
Yes — DESCRIBE VOLUME is exactly how you inspect metadata about a Unity Catalog volume in Databricks.

